<ToolPage
  kind="guide"
  title="How Houston Fire's Debit Day System Actually Works"
  description="HFD's A/B/C/D shifts, the 8-day cycle, and the A/C3-style debit notation decoded — how partner shifts, 9 debit groups, and the 72-day duty cycle turn a light calendar into contract hours."
  h1="Houston Fire's Debit Days, Decoded"
>
  <p>
    Look at the official Houston Fire Department calendar and the first thing you notice is the
    strange notation: <strong>A/C3</strong>, <strong>B/D5</strong>, <strong>C/A7</strong>. The
    letter before the slash is easy — that shift is on duty. The rest is the
    <em>debit day system</em>, and it is the part of HFD's schedule that confuses every rookie and
    most of their families.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with the base cycle</h2>
  <p>
    HFD runs four shifts — A, B, C, D — on 24-hour tours starting at 6:30 am, repeating an 8-day
    sequence: A, B, A, B, C, D, C, D. Each shift works two tours in eight days (tour, day off,
    tour, five days off). Two tours per eight days is only about 42 hours a week — noticeably
    lighter than the ~46.7 hours HFD firefighters are actually scheduled to work under contract.
    The gap has to come from somewhere. It comes from debit days.
  </p>

  <h2>The partner-shift mechanism</h2>
  <p>
    Shifts are paired: <strong>A with C, B with D</strong>. On any duty day, the on-duty shift is
    joined by one <em>debit group</em> from its partner shift — a subset of the partner's members
    working an extra 24-hour tour on what would otherwise be a day off. Each shift's members are
    divided into nine debit groups, numbered 1 through 9, and the calendar notation names which
    group owes the extra tour: "A/C3" means A shift is on duty and C shift's group 3 works with
    them.
  </p>

  <h2>The 72-day duty cycle</h2>
  <p>
    Nine debit groups rotating across a shift-pair's duty days produce a 72-day super-cycle (9
    groups × the 8-day base cycle), which the official calendar marks with "End/Begin 72 Day Duty
    Cycle" notes. Practically: your debit day comes around roughly every nine of your shift's duty
    days — about once every five to six weeks — and it is fully predictable years ahead, because
    the whole machine is deterministic. One extra 24-hour tour per cycle is what lifts the average
    from 42 toward contract hours.
  </p>

  <h2>What this means for planning</h2>
  <p>
    Two practical consequences. First, your five-day breaks are not all equal — one break per
    debit rotation loses a day to the extra tour, so trips booked months out should check the debit
    calendar, not just the base cycle. Second, trades involve two currencies: regular tours trade
    within your shift, debit obligations trade within your debit group, and mixing them up is the
    classic scheduling argument at the kitchen table. Know which kind of day you are giving away.
  </p>

  <h2>Your personal HFD calendar</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/houston-fire-department-shift-calendar">HFD shift calendar</a> on this site is
    aligned to the city's official rotation (which has run unbroken since at least 2022) — pick
    your shift letter and get every base tour for 12 months, holidays answered, exportable to your
    phone. Debit groups are member-specific, so confirm your group number at the station and pencil
    those days on top. For the wider context of how Houston's system compares with other
    departments' rotations, see the <a href="/firefighter-shift-calendar">firefighter calendar
    hub</a> and the <a href="/blog/48-96-vs-kelly">48/96 vs Kelly comparison</a>.
  </p>
